.\\n* Dango & Mochi: Sweet, chewy rice cakes on skewers.\\n* Matcha Soft Serve: Rich green tea ice cream.\\n\\n---\\n\\nBUDGET TIPS FOR STUDENTS\\n\\n* Stay in Hostels: Book dorm beds or capsule hotels. They are super clean, safe, and cost a fraction of hotel rooms.\\n* Eat at Convenience Stores: Konbini (7-Eleven, FamilyMart, Lawson) have fresh meals, sandwiches, and bento boxes for under 500 yen (approx. 280 INR).\\n* Save on Long-Distance Travel: Instead of the expensive national JR Pass, use night buses (like Willer Express) between Tokyo and Kyoto to save on both travel and a night of accommodation.\\n* Free Water: Tap water across Japan is completely safe to drink. Carry a reusable bottle.\\n* Free Attractions: Many temples, parks, and viewpoints (like the Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building) are completely free.\\n* Discount Shopping: Buy snacks and souvenirs at Daiso (100-yen shop) or Don Quijote (discount mega-store).\\n* Carry Cash: While cards work in big stores, small food stalls, temple ticket counters, and train recharge machines often take cash only.\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"custom\":{}}],\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"20-DAY JAPAN TRIP ITINERARY FOR STUDENTS\\n\\nTRIP ROUTE\\nTokyo - Hakone & Mt. Fuji - Kyoto - Nara - Osaka - Hiroshima - Tokyo\\n\\n---\\n\\nDAY-WISE PLAN\\n\\nDay 1: Arrival in Tokyo\\n* Fly from India to Tokyo (Narita or Haneda Airport).\\n* Pick up your pocket Wi-Fi and IC travel card (Suica or Pasmo).\\n* Check in to your hostel and rest.\\n\\nDay 2: Modern Tokyo\\n* Walk across the famous Shibuya Crossing.\\n* Take a photo with the Hachiko Statue.\\n* Explore trendy youth fashion on Takeshita Street in Harajuku.\\n* Visit the quiet Meiji Shrine.\\n\\nDay 3: Traditional Tokyo and Anime Hub\\n* Visit Senso-ji Temple in Asakusa.\\n* Shop for small souvenirs along Nakamise Street.\\n* Spend the afternoon in Akihabara, the center of anime and video games.\\n\\nDay 4: City Views and Gardens\\